22:20:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, the poles genuinely are a special case—this isn't a manufactured complexity on my part. Here's the concrete issue: **Lines of latitude are circles.** East and west are the directions you travel *along* a line of latitude. At every other point on Earth, you're standing on some latitude circle, and moving along it in one direction is east, the other is west. But at the exact pole, you're not on a latitude circle—you're at the *single point* where every latitude circle shrinks to zero radius. All lines of longitude converge there. So the concept of "moving along a line of latitude" breaks down: there is no latitude circle to move along. **Concrete illustration:** Stand exactly at the North Pole. Every direction you can walk is south (since south is "toward the equator/away from the pole" and that's true no matter which way you step). There's no "walk east" option distinct from "walk south," because east/west require a latitude line, and you're not on one—you're at the pole itself. This is also why maps and globes always show the poles as a single point (or a stretched line in some projections)—they're mathematically singular for coordinate purposes, the same way the tip of a cone is singular. So it's not pedantry—it's a real geometric fact: **on** the pole (not just near it), east and west are undefined, only north/south (or "toward/away from pole") make sense. That's why I gave the "direction of rotation" convention—it's the accepted workaround for *assigning* an east/west label there, since strict geometry doesn't supply one.
